WEBVTT

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.370
We're late enough already. They've closed the doors.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:10.410
It's a medical school lecture. I want to see what the attraction is.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:11.430
The psychology of crime.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:11.810
What?

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.230
Psychology of crime. Come.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.660
I have a good mind to stay.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:15.540
Who's the lecturer?

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:20.720
I'd stay if I were you.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:22.200
He only lectures once a week.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:24.310
Now you've got to stay.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:37.770
Up to this point in the present series of lectures, we've dealt exclusively with abnormal mentalities.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:44.830
I emphasize the fact that in civilized communities, 80% of our murderers and violent criminals

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:51.290
were those whose minds had been conditioned by exceptional nervous stress and unhealthy environment.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:57.210
Last Friday, we dealt with the smaller group of strictly moronic criminals.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.960
And now we come to that much more...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.920
interesting phenomenon the same criminal the man who is prepared to pursue his own ethical

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:13.340
convictions even to the point of murder the man whose punishment is apt to weigh heavily on the

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.820
conscience of society because his actions as likely as not have been inspired by just as

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:24.400
great an integrity as those of the men who sit in judgment at worst he's an irresponsible

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.840
opportunist at the best he's a man with a strong sense of

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.600
Justice, even a mystic.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.500
I propose to relate the case history of a murderer of this class.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.710
A perfectly sane, valuable member of society.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.780
Better give him a fictitious name.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:48.030
Give them all fictitious names, all the characters in this case.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:49.900
He was a surgeon.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.300
They called him Mr. Joyce, Mr. Michael Joyce.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.060
He was well thought of in the medical profession

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.660
and had a house of his own in Harley Street.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.360
His marriage had been an unhappy one, and he'd lived apart from his wife for several years.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.190
Consequently, he threw all his energy and interest into his work.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.340
As a brain specialist, he operated in three London hospitals

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:17.380
and had developed a technique which considerably reduced the mortality rate in this field.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.890
He was reserved in his personal relationships and had no close friends.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:28.040
His only relaxation was to sit at home and play the piano or go to an occasional concert.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:29.980
Otherwise...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.600
it was a life devoted solely to work.

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:35.470
A life that by normal standards was unutterably lonely and empty.

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:38.350
But he never recognised this himself

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.270
until he met a woman called Emma Wright.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:43.580
When she first came into his consulting room,

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:44.920
he hardly even noticed her.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:47.780
She was just an ordinary middle-class woman

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.740
who happened to have a daughter who was going blind.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:51.140
Sit down, Mrs. Wright.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:54.360
Let's have a look at this head of yours.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.020
I gather it was an emergency operation after an air raid.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:04:59.810
Yes.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:01.660
Now the eyesight's affected.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.530
The eye specialist said there was nothing he could do.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:06.730
Can you see well enough to read?

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:07.810
Not really.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.100
I have the eye specialist report.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.380
Early optic atrophy.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:16.990
This is a fairly serious condition, Mrs. Wright.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.380
The thing for us to do is to take her into hospital,

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.100
make a thorough investigation so as to establish the exact cause.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:26.250
You wouldn't mind that, Anne, would you? Would you?

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:27.390
Will it hurt?

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.450
No. We'll take good care of you.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.160
Do you want her to go in right away?

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:32.300
I think she should.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:34.370
Anne, come over here.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.530
Sit down.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.450
We don't want the atrophy to become too far advanced.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:43.540
Anne, I'm just going to look into your eyes.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.260
You see those two figures on the mantelpiece?

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:46.140
Keep your eye on them.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:49.580
My face will get in between, but don't look at me.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.670
The personality of the child's mother

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.480
remained for the time being a matter of no great importance.

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:59.450
But after the child had been submitted to a series of tests...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.100
and X-rays had proved that her trouble was due to a small foreign body lodged anteriorly to the optic chiasma.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.550
It became obvious that a fairly serious operation was necessary,

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:12.380
and the relationship between these two became inevitably less remote.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.440
Will it be very dangerous, the operation?

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:20.610
To her life, there's always a risk with a major operation.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.080
How great a risk?

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.340
The mortality rate with this operation is 1%.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:27.720
And if you don't operate?

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:29.760
Go blind.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.370
If only my husband were here, he'd know what to do.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:38.330
I'd hate to go ahead without him.

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:41.290
We might consider putting it off for a short time if he's coming home soon.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:43.430
Not for several months, I'm afraid.

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:45.290
In that case, I shouldn't advise it.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.500
Every week that we let it go, it gets progressively worse.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:54.790
I know. I know you're right, but you don't think she...

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:58.650
I mean, she couldn't be among the 1%.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:06:59.980
My man, there's no question.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.680
at all i've met this problem before on many occasions always with complete success i wish

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:14.090
you felt you could trust me it isn't that i'll do what you say well i suggest we leave anne here

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:20.400
at the hospital she's comfortable and i'll operate as soon as it can be arranged all right

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:27.260
there's nothing to it anne we give you something nice to make you go to sleep and when you wake up

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:29.970
again it's all over and you'll be able to see

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.690
properly again oh mommy they want to cut my hair off but darling it'll grow again oh must i stay

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:41.400
here mommy mr joyce will take great care of you oh don't go please mommy how would you like your

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.770
mother to stay with you till you go to sleep can't you stay with me until i wake up again

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:52.390
she can stay with you all the time if she likes oh yes please mommy yes you can all right darling

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:56.100
i'll stay i'll see you later anne i won't be a minute

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:46.220
The surgeon who is about to operate is often as nervous as a prima donna.

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:51.290
This was exaggerated in the present case by the feverish devotion of the child's mother.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.740
Invaded itself to Michael Joyce.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.650
Robbed him of that cold detachment which is the doctor's great strength.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:09:59.900
Never before.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:04.370
had he been so acutely aware of the identity of the piece of human material he was working on

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:10.760
as he took the scalpel and made the first incision and as the sister handed him the artery forceps.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.570
...anesthetic easily, and body was located...

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:09.810
The chief sufferer, of course, was the wretched woman

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:12.310
who'd been pacing up and down the waiting room for two hours.

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:15.960
She broke down as soon as Michael Joyce came in and made his report,

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:19.640
and only pulled herself together when she remembered rather guiltily

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.500
the promise she'd made to Anne to stay with her throughout the operation.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:26.580
He was anxious to get back to her before she came out of the anaesthetic.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:29.980
There was another woman...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.250
in the room with her, whom she introduced as her sister-in-law, a Mrs. Catherine Howard,

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:38.860
a rather overdressed young lady who seemed to have very little interest in Anne's welfare.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:45.340
They had to wait some weeks to find out whether the operation had actually succeeded in saving Anne's eyesight.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:49.540
Meanwhile, they shared the daily uncertainties and anxieties.

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:54.780
Emma Wright depended more and more on the feeling of confidence which he gave her.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.960
When it became obvious that Anne was on her way to recovery, she was tremendously grateful to her.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:03.090
A feeling which Michael Joyce did not mistake,

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:06.590
but he knew that his own interest in her was greater than it should be.

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:12.080
Without admitting it to himself, he began to dread the day when the job would be completed,

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:14.480
the day of the final examination,

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:18.020
after which Anne and her mother were to return to their home in the country.

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.720
Anne and I went to pictures last night, the first time for over a year.

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:22.660
It was all coloured.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:28.380
Well, I don't suppose we shall see you again?

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:29.980
I hope you will.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.950
I hope so, too.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:34.570
Not professionally, of course.

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:35.910
The sun's come out, Mummy.

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:37.640
We're going to the park, shall we?

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:39.160
Goodbye.

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:41.670
Are you really going to the park?

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:42.490
Yes, why?

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:44.720
Do you mind if I go with you?

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:50.060
But oughtn't you to tell someone you're going in?

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:54.180
Tell them when I get back, then there can be no mistake about it.

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:59.350
You should have said goodbye when their relationship came to its natural conclusion.

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:01.470
but he couldn't bring himself to.

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:05.010
She represented all the things that were so painfully lacking

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:07.770
from a life devoted to textbooks and operating theatres.

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:10.390
She was so simple and unaffected.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:13.860
She told him about Philip, her husband,

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:18.060
how his work as a geologist had kept them apart for years at a time,

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:21.240
and now he was going to give it up so that they could be together.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:24.390
She told him how they'd hoped for more children.

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:28.770
Having only one, she'd become all the more nervous and possessive about Anne.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.920
They went on seeing each other and found they had the same tastes.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:38.240
They both liked music and fell into the habit of going to concerts together.

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:42.030
Sometimes she'd come and play the piano at his house.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.060
Can you drive out all this way?

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:28.060
I really should have stayed in town.

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:29.910
Only I hate leaving Anne alone at night.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:31.740
I'm relying on you to show me the way.

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:33.560
You don't know this part of the country.

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:34.810
Oh, I know the road.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:35.460
Good.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:37.740
Wait a minute.

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:40.240
And we lost?

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:42.150
Oh, it's all right.

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:42.910
That's our chapel.

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:44.180
Your chapel?

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:45.920
Yes, you can see it from my bedroom window.

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:47.040
Draw up when we come to it.

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:47.760
I must show it to you.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:50.660
There used to be great eruptions in the family about it.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.400
When the wind's in our direction, you can hear them singing.

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:08.740
I rather like it.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:14.160
The sound drifts across and, I don't know, it's rather peaceful.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:16.260
Some people hate it.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:18.120
Kate was always trying to get Philip to sell the house.

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:19.580
That was before she was married, of course.

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:22.720
Then after she came back here, after her husband was killed,

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:24.150
she started all over again.

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:26.560
She says Clay always plays out of tune.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:27.400
Who's Clay?

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:29.000
Our gardener. He plays the organ here.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.470
Kate thinks we ought to get rid of him because of it.

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:33.050
Why, does it affect his gardening?

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:34.060
Oh, no.

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.780
It's just that Kate thinks that if he hadn't a job,

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:37.940
he might go and live in another district,

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:39.460
and then there wouldn't be anyone to play the organ.

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:40.100
Who's Kate?

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:41.280
My sister-in-law.

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:43.020
You met her one day at the hospital.

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:43.600
Did I?

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:46.660
You even remember the people you meet?

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:48.990
I remember the important ones,

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:51.060
the ones I want to remember.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:55.390
That's our house up there.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.740
There's something I've been wanting to ask you all the evening.

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:03.320
Yes.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:05.860
It's just, are you divorced?

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:08.230
No.

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:10.750
Diana doesn't want a divorce.

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:13.550
Why do you ask?

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:15.580
No reason.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:16.820
I just wondered.

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:18.260
We ought to be going.

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:19.100
It must be awfully late.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:29.880
Okay.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.400
up to London for the day. She made a habit of calling in at Michael's house in Harley Street

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:50.410
after the day shopping and often they'd have dinner together afterwards. I had to keep you

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:53.750
waiting. I got messed up with my appointments this afternoon. It doesn't matter. Come and listen.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:59.660
It's a record Anne's made. It almost sounds as though she were a real pianist.

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:02.460
I'm trying to think what this is.

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:03.810
Madam, will you walk?

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:06.770
Good, is that really Anne?

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:08.350
Of course it is.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:09.410
Quite a professional job.

00:17:09.610 --> 00:17:09.710
Shh.

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:15.890
That was a mistake.

00:17:16.090 --> 00:17:16.950
She always goes wrong there,

00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:18.750
but we're going to have another one made without any mistakes.

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:24.690
Can play it, really.

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:26.080
This is where she goes wrong.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:38.060
She used to play very well.

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:41.140
After the accident, of course, she had to play from memory.

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:43.280
I made her go on practicing.

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:46.460
Emma, do you love your husband?

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:51.970
Well, do you?

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:55.850
I don't know how to answer that question.

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:57.360
Do you know why I asked?

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:04.730
Yes. Well, Michael, I don't know what to say.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:07.000
Philip and I have been together for so long.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:08.940
I love you, Emma.

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:12.600
You shouldn't have told me.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:17.000
If we'd kept quiet about it, we might have gone on seeing each other.

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:19.170
It wouldn't have worked.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:23.170
I suppose not.

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:25.070
I wanted you to know.

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:28.440
I did know, Michael.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:38.900
I tried not to recognize it and tried to tell myself that something would happen to make it all right.

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:41.060
Then I knew nothing could.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:45.890
It can never be all right. Neither of us is free or ever likely to be.

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:48.500
There's nothing we can do about it.

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:51.190
We won't see each other again after today.

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:59.560
No. Oh, Michael, so shall I.

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:29.960
for them to enter us

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:41.810
No, neither was happy about it. Emma's husband was giving up the work he liked so as to be with her, and she was torn between her loyalty towards him and her love for Michael Joyce.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:48.820
Finally, she decided to write to her husband and explain what had happened to ask him to release her.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:19:59.870
Send it, can I?

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:01.380
No, you can't.

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:04.280
It would be too unfair.

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:07.340
It's Anne I'm thinking of, of course.

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:08.580
Well, that's your answer.

00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:10.640
Certainly wouldn't be fair to her.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.380
There's only one thing for us to do, I'm afraid.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:19.140
Bye, Michael.

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:27.210
No, don't come near me.

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:29.970
We must never see you.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:30.700
Never did see each other again.

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:47.930
Never did see each other again.

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:11.230
Good morning, Mr. Joyce.

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:12.580
Good morning.

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:14.670
About Mrs. Wright.

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:17.660
Right.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:20.180
Remember, the model's a little girl.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:22.820
What?

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:24.360
Oh, will you come in?

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:29.750
Seek in here, please.

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:34.040
What about her?

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:38.220
She's right. She fell out of a window and broke her neck.

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:56.050
Established at the inquest, fatal fall took place at about six o'clock in the evening.

00:21:57.460 --> 00:21:59.960
The only other people in the house at the time were the child and...

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.470
and a housemaid who testified that earlier in the afternoon mrs catherine howard had visited the

00:22:06.470 --> 00:22:13.880
house did you see mrs howard leave yes sir i saw her get into a car and drive away at about what

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:19.350
time a little before six i should say sir and it was some minutes later that you heard a noise

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:23.780
as of someone falling yes that will be all miss bond thank you

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:41.900
Repeat after me, I swear by Almighty God, that I will speak the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth.

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:47.180
You are Mrs. Catherine Hart? Yes. And your address is? I live at the Arcadia Hotel.

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:53.220
What was your relationship to the deceased? She was my sister-in-law. She was married to my brother Philip.

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:59.910
Did you last see Mrs. Wright alive? About six o'clock on the evening of the accident. I was with her for about...

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.970
You had an engagement with her?

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:06.940
Well, not exactly an engagement, but she knew that I might stop by.

00:23:08.120 --> 00:23:10.760
Was she actually expecting you to stop by?

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:13.060
Well, she wasn't exactly expecting me.

00:23:13.710 --> 00:23:17.510
But since my husband was killed, I've been in the habit of popping in and out whenever I was nearby.

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:19.250
What happened when you arrived?

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:20.700
Nothing.

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:22.270
Did you talk?

00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:24.300
Yes, we chatted for a while.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:25.970
About anything in particular?

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:27.880
No, just talk.

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:29.830
Did she seem worried?

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:34.390
On the contrary. She was very cheerful. She was looking forward to her husband coming home.

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:37.240
Had she been suffering from ill health?

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:38.040
Not at all.

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:41.920
Then there was nothing in her manner to suggest that anything was wrong.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:45.000
Certainly not. Oh, but she did have a great fear of heights.

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:47.210
She had a great fear of heights?

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:50.740
Do you mean that she mentioned it on this particular afternoon?

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:52.720
Well, no.

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:54.990
Why did you mention it just now?

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:58.690
Well, it seemed to me the only possible explanation for her falling out of the window.

00:23:59.370 --> 00:23:59.650
I see.

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:03.450
What was Mrs. Wright doing when you left her?

00:24:03.970 --> 00:24:07.440
She was in her room. I think she was going to turn out her stocking drawer.

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:10.340
Thank you, Mrs. Howard. That will be all.

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:25.780
Come over here by me.

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:35.440
Now, Anne, you understand what is meant by telling the truth, don't you?

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:36.300
Yes.

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:40.540
Very important that you do, because I'm going to ask you a few questions

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:42.340
and I want you to answer them truthfully.

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:45.880
Tell me, when did you last see your mother?

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:49.600
It was a little while before I went to bed.

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:52.220
Where was your mother?

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:53.860
In her room.

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:56.360
Did you go in to talk to her?

00:24:58.160 --> 00:24:59.220
I went to say goodnight.

00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:01.450
Did you say goodnight?

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:02.680
Yes.

00:25:04.220 --> 00:25:06.080
Did your mother seem normal?

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:08.880
Yes.

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:13.640
Tell me, Anne, was there anyone with your mother?

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:25.850
Nothing happened seemed unusual to you.

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:26.420
No.

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:27.770
Thank you.

00:25:37.540 --> 00:25:38.460
Repeat after me.

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:40.440
I swear by Almighty God

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:43.140
that I will speak the truth

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:44.300
the whole truth

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:45.940
and nothing but the truth.

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:49.590
Dr. Reynolds, is your name William Gaunt Reynolds?

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:51.600
Do you practice in Darnborough?

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:53.700
Were you called in this case?

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:56.040
What time did you get there?

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:49.980
Will you be in to dinner tonight, sir?

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:53.060
No, I've got to go out.

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:54.050
Very good, sir.

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:02.200
The Arcadia Hotel.

00:27:04.470 --> 00:27:05.770
The Arcadia Hotel.

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:45.310
This is Catherine Howard in.

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:48.140
The party's in Mrs. Devis' suite, room 29.

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:48.880
Party?

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:52.000
I'm sorry, sir. I thought perhaps you were one of the guests.

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:53.800
Yes, yes, I am.

00:27:54.290 --> 00:27:56.140
Room 29, second floor.

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:14.170
Thank you.

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:20.750
Oh, I'm so glad you were able to come.

00:28:20.950 --> 00:28:23.110
We're in such a muddle. Everybody seems to be here.

00:28:23.450 --> 00:28:24.630
I won't have to introduce you.

00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:26.130
Oh, look, there's Joan Scott Potter.

00:28:26.390 --> 00:28:28.470
Joan, I want you to meet Mr...

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:28.890
How do you do?

00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:29.590
Albert.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.200
But I can't bear to see people without it in their hands.

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:32.940
There.

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:33.800
Thank you.

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:36.200
Oh, dear, I might have known she'd turn up.

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:39.020
Oh, darling, I'm so glad you were able to come.

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:42.900
These are the ones we ought to avoid.

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:44.270
All right, what's wrong with them?

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:47.030
After Jenny Deaver's last party, Judy Hammond went blind.

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:48.080
Permanently?

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:49.860
No, she's over there somewhere.

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:52.920
There's Kate Howard, and on the day of the inquest.

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:54.400
But she'd do it.

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:55.950
Put this down for me somewhere, will you?

00:28:56.500 --> 00:28:56.780
Yes.

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:07.000
Being very unsociable.

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:10.140
If there's someone here I know you'll adore, she's longing to meet you.

00:29:10.560 --> 00:29:12.280
Sylvia, darling, you don't know Peter, do you?

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:13.600
I'm dying to meet you.

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:14.680
Got a drink? Good.

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:16.260
What on earth is that waiter doing?

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:21.200
Is your name really Sylvia?

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:22.950
What's wrong with Sylvia?

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:24.780
Nothing at all, only mine isn't Peter.

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:27.100
Excuse me, I must take this drink to somebody.

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:29.830
I just love that hair. Hello.

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:34.380
Why, hello, Doctor. I never expected to find you at a cocktail party.

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:36.020
I hardly expected to find myself at one.

00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:37.060
Have you been here long?

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:38.980
No, I've only just arrived. You're looking very well.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:41.540
Oh, I'm in absolute dread. I've been having the most awful time.

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:44.840
I expect you read about it. My sister-in-law, Emma Wright, you know,

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:46.120
she fell out of a window and got killed.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:47.380
Yes, I did hear about it.

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:48.860
I've just come straight from the inquest.

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:51.960
I wonder if I dare try one of these. I shall not think what's in it.

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:56.580
Catherine, you poor dear. What really happened? Did you throw herself out today?

00:29:56.720 --> 00:29:57.880
No, of course she didn't.

00:29:58.040 --> 00:29:59.980
Is one of those going begging? I'll die if I don't...

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:03.120
I'm afraid this belongs to Mrs. Hart. You might find one over there, though.

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:05.960
Now, don't go away, Catherine. I simply must hear it all.

00:30:08.290 --> 00:30:09.650
You saved my life.

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:11.850
I think you ought to keep moving if you don't want her to catch you again.

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:13.050
Quite a good idea.

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:19.720
Dear, you must give me the lowdown.

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:22.770
My husband swears that someone pushed her out of the window,

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:23.970
and it's all being hushed out.

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.780
Come in the corner in the quiet. I can't bear it.

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:28.740
I can't. Haven't you got to telephone your mother?

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:29.860
Huh?

00:30:32.810 --> 00:30:34.950
See you later, dear. Excuse me.

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:40.380
Kate, my dear, you're not going.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:42.740
Darling, I must. They're too gruesome.

00:30:42.980 --> 00:30:44.060
You're staying here for dinner.

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:45.620
Well, don't rely on me.

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:48.080
Oh, quick, there's that awful woman. See you later, dear.

00:30:48.280 --> 00:30:49.040
You're a menace.

00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:49.800
Catherine!

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:51.800
Darling, you haven't got a drink.

00:30:52.040 --> 00:30:52.440
No.

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:11.270
Thank you.

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:12.240
Thank you.

00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:15.800
I'm really not suitably dressed to be here.

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:17.060
I ought to have changed, I suppose,

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:18.400
but I've had such a hectic week,

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:19.180
what with the inquest

00:31:19.180 --> 00:31:19.940
and everyone ringing up

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:21.480
and asking silly questions about Emma.

00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:22.840
I do sympathise with you.

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:23.790
From all I know,

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:25.050
she's left me with a load of debts.

00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:27.180
Was Mrs. Wright in debt?

00:31:27.620 --> 00:31:28.540
Oh, not hers, mine.

00:31:28.800 --> 00:31:29.960
I wonder if they've got...

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:32.270
What's happened to Anne?

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:33.140
Anne?

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:35.310
How do you know Anne?

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:36.550
I operated on her.

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:37.960
Why, yes, of course.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:38.480
So you did.

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:39.300
How silly of me.

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:41.430
You know, I think I have one too many at that party.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:43.430
I almost forgot, for the moment, who you were.

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:44.700
Well, what has happened to her?

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:46.050
Oh, she's gone to Portsmouth.

00:31:46.630 --> 00:31:48.110
Emma's mother has a house down there.

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:50.800
There's a dreary old miser, whoever there was one.

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:52.300
She hates me.

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:53.670
As if you cared.

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:54.610
I do care.

00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:55.430
I'm most interested.

00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:57.170
You're just being very polite and sweet.

00:31:57.640 --> 00:31:58.740
Frankly, I'm bad.

00:31:58.740 --> 00:31:59.880
Please, please, please.

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:02.260
You'll never know what she's going to put in them.

00:32:02.550 --> 00:32:04.170
It'll pass off as soon as you've had something to eat.

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:06.010
Tell me about the house, what's happened to that?

00:32:06.610 --> 00:32:07.230
What house?

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:08.000
Mrs. Wright.

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:09.390
Oh, it's up for sale.

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:10.460
Already?

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:12.740
Well, we moved Anne out the night her mother died.

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:14.220
Philip will never want to see the place again.

00:32:14.610 --> 00:32:15.190
It's empty now.

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:16.640
You got any potted shrimps?

00:32:17.210 --> 00:32:17.870
Afraid not, madam.

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:18.950
Lobster cocktails?

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:19.690
Yes, madam.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:31.900
Be very forward of me to hope that I shall see you again.

00:32:32.270 --> 00:32:34.830
If you'd held your breath a moment longer, I should have suggested it myself.

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:35.960
Well?

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:39.270
Well, are you free tomorrow evening?

00:32:39.470 --> 00:32:40.390
I'll see that I am.

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:41.680
Right, yeah.

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:44.620
Same place, six o'clock, in the bar?

00:32:45.070 --> 00:32:45.470
Wonderful.

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:46.150
Good.

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:46.740
Good night.

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:48.080
Good night.

